    \field{abstract}{%
    We present a study of the superconducting penetration depth \${$\lambda\$$}
  in aluminum thin films of varying thickness. The range of thicknesses chosen
  spans from the thin-film regime to the regime approaching bulk behavior. The
  penetration depths observed range from \${$\lambda$}= 163.3\textbackslash
  pm0.4\textasciitilde\textbackslash rm\textbraceleft nm\textbraceright\$ for
  the thinnest \$20\textasciitilde\textbackslash rm\textbraceleft
  nm\textbraceright\$ samples down to \${$\lambda$}= 53.6\textbackslash
  pm0.4\textasciitilde\textbackslash rm\textbraceleft nm\textbraceright\$ for
  the \$200\textasciitilde\textbackslash rm\textbraceleft
  nm\textbraceright\$-thick ones. In order to accurately determine
  \${$\lambda\$$}, we performed complementary measurements using the frequency
  of superconducting \$LC\$ resonators as well as the resistance of
  normal-state meanders. Both methods yield comparable results, providing a
  well-characterized set of values of \${$\lambda\$$} in aluminum in the
  relevant range for applications in fields such as quantum computing and
  microwave radiation detector technologies.%
    }
