    \field{abstract}{%
    Hexagonal semiconductors such as 4H SiC have important high-frequency,
  high-power, and high-temperature applications. The applications require
  accurate knowledge of both ordinary and extraordinary relative
  permittivities, {$\varepsilon$} and {$\varepsilon\vert\vert$}, perpendicular
  and parallel, respectively, to the c axis of these semiconductors. However,
  due to challenges for suitable test setups and precision high-frequency
  measurements, little reliable data exists for these semiconductors especially
  at millimeter-wave frequencies. Recently, we reported
  {$\varepsilon\vert\vert$} of 4H SiC from 110 to 170 GHz. This paper expands
  on the previous report to include both {$\varepsilon$} and
  {$\varepsilon\vert\vert$} of the same material from 55 to 330 GHz, as well as
  their temperature and humidity dependence enabled by improving the
  measurement precision to two decimal points. For example, at room
  temperature, real {$\varepsilon$} and {$\varepsilon\vert\vert$} are constant
  at 9.77 \textpm{} 0.01 and 10.20 \textpm{} 0.05, respectively. By contrast,
  the ordinary loss tangent increases linearly with the frequency f in the form
  of (4.9 \textpm{} 0.1) \texttimes{} 10-16 f. The loss tangent, less than 1
  \texttimes{} 10-4 over most millimeter-wave frequencies, is significantly
  lower than that of sapphire, our previous low-loss standard. Finally, both
  {$\varepsilon$} and {$\varepsilon\vert\vert$} have weak temperature
  coefficients on the order of 10-4 /{$^\circ$}C. The knowledge reported here
  is especially critical to millimeter-wave applications of 4H SiC, not only
  for solid-state devices and circuits, but also as windows for high-power
  vacuum electronics.%
    }
